NASA Anomaly detection 

Overview
This project demonstrates how a machine learning  anomaly detection script can be containerized with Docker, deployed with Kubernetes, and accessed via a Flask API in the browser. 

1. ML Script
Runs anomaly detection on NASA turbofan engine degradation simulation data using Isolation Forest algorithm. This algorithm was chosen as it is good for high dimensional data without the assumption of normal distribution and is unsupervised. 
The results are saved inside /app/output.
The Flask API is started on port 8000 and exposes results via the http endpoint.

2. Docker builds a container image based on dockerfile with Python + dependencies as listed in requirements.txt and runs the ML script inside the container.

3. Kubernetes : Minikube (local Kubernetes cluster in a VM / local simulator of the Kubernetes system) provides a single node Kubernetes cluster.

4. Flask API


************************************************************
Components : 
    * Pod - wraps around the container
    * Deployment - Ensures pod runs and controls replicas, resource requests and container definition
    * Node - A worker machine. Minikube creates one node. Runs kubelet(*sadfcdf?) + container runtime (*fdfsdfsd?)
    * Persistent volume (PV) - provides storage
    * Persistent Volume Claim (PVC) - Requests storage from PV
    * Service - Exposes pod to the outside world. Pods get dynamic IPs. Service provides stable way to reach pods.  In this case Flask app is exposed at a fixed port in the Minikube node. It bridges the app running in the pod to the external world.

    Kubernetes Overview (Found below **)
**********************************************************

Project Structure
nasa-anomaly/
|----CMAPSS_IsolationForest.py
|----DataReadme.txt
|----Dockerfile 
|----README
|----requirements.txt
|----test_FD001.txt
|----train_FD001.txt
|----k8s/
    |----kind/
    |----manifests/
        |----deployment.yaml
        |----pv.yaml
        |----pvc.yaml
        |----service.yaml
    |----minikube/
        |----deploy_minikube.sh

* Dockerfile
    Defines the container environment using python:3.9-slim as the base image.
        The Dockerfile has comments included for every execution

* requirements.txt list all dependencies in PyPI version only

* train_FD001.txt   
    Holds the training data

* test_FD001.txt
    Hold the test data

* DataReadme
    Information on training and test data

*ouptut
    *TestAnomalyResults
        Anomalies predicted in test data
    *TrainAnomalyResults
        Anomalies predicted in train data
    *plots/
        Plots with anomalies and correct data points

* CMAPSS_IsolationForest.py
    ML script to train the model and classify the data points




    

Kubernetes workflow
+--------------------------+
|   ML Script (Flask) |
|      port 8000      |
+------------+-------------+
             |
             v
+--------------------------+
|      Docker Container    |
|  Built via Dockerfile    |
+------------+-------------+
             |
             v
+--------------------------+
|         Pod              |
| (smallest unit in K8s)   |
+------------+-------------+
             |
             v
+--------------------------+
|      Node (Minikube VM)  |            
+------------+-------------+
             |
             v
+--------------------------+
|       Kubernetes Service |
| Type: NodePort           |
| Example: 30080 -> 8000   |
+------------+-------------+
             |
             v
+--------------------------+      +------------------+
|  Minikube Cluster IP     | ---> |   Host Browser   |
|  (minikube ip)           |      | http://IP:30080 |     #dclusgdlgc verify
+--------------------------+      +------------------+

Volume Flow
ML Script (/app/output)  
      ↓
Container VolumeMount (/app/output)  
      ↓
Pod Volume (PVC claimName: ml-output-pvc)  
      ↓
Persistent Volume (PV → hostPath: /mnt/data/ml-output)  
      ↓
Mounted via Minikube (--mount-string="${PWD}/output:/mnt/data/ml-output")  
      ↓
Host Folder (./output)

